WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.872
Task statement 1.2.

2
00:00:01.872 --> 00:00:06.712
Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns.

3
00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:16.031
A research system that sends every question to every subagent wastes time on simple queries and still misses angles on hard ones.

4
00:00:17.431 --> 00:00:21.602
A multi-agent system in this exam is shaped like a hub and spokes.

5
00:00:21.602 --> 00:00:25.521
One coordinator sits in the middle and hands out focused jobs.

6
00:00:25.521 --> 00:00:31.905
Each subagent does its piece and reports back to the coordinator, never sideways to another subagent.

7
00:00:31.905 --> 00:00:37.847
Think of a news editor who assigns stories to reporters and then puts the front page together.

8
00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:41.146
The coordinator has three jobs.

9
00:00:41.146 --> 00:00:50.521
It routes work to the right subagents, it handles failures when one of them comes back empty or broken, and it combines everything into the final answer.

10
00:00:50.521 --> 00:00:54.564
Remember that each subagent starts with a clean, isolated context.

11
00:00:54.564 --> 00:01:00.324
It only knows what the coordinator tells it, which is exactly why the hub must stay in charge.

12
00:01:01.724 --> 00:01:04.742
Good coordinators size the effort to the question.

13
00:01:04.742 --> 00:01:10.837
A single fact needs one agent, a comparison needs a few, and a broad research question may need many.

14
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:15.484
Give each subagent a clearly separate scope so they do not repeat each other.

15
00:01:15.484 --> 00:01:19.045
Then, before writing anything, check what is still missing.

16
00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:22.545
A split that is too narrow drops topics without any error.

17
00:01:23.945 --> 00:01:29.815
In the research scenario, the coordinator splits the housing question into four separate scopes.

18
00:01:29.815 --> 00:01:37.457
When the results come back, it notices that rural areas are not covered at all, so it sends one more search before synthesis.

19
00:01:37.457 --> 00:01:40.208
Later, a user asks a quick factual follow-up.

20
00:01:40.208 --> 00:01:45.833
The coordinator sends that to a single search agent instead of rerunning the whole pipeline.

21
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:56.957
On the exam, be suspicious of any design that runs the full pipeline every time, lets subagents chat directly, or blames the last agent for missing coverage.

22
00:01:56.957 --> 00:02:01.912
When a final report has holes, look first at how the coordinator split the work.

23
00:02:01.912 --> 00:02:08.353
And if two subagents keep finding the same sources, the fix is clearer scope boundaries in their briefs.

24
00:02:09.753 --> 00:02:10.342
To recap.

25
00:02:10.342 --> 00:02:16.034
The coordinator is the hub that routes work, handles failures and assembles the answer.

26
00:02:16.034 --> 00:02:20.287
Subagents start with isolated context and report only to the hub.

27
00:02:20.287 --> 00:02:23.231
Pick the subagents each query actually needs.

28
00:02:23.231 --> 00:02:29.316
And split scope cleanly, look for gaps, and fill them before you synthesize the final result.

